From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: an unknown conn_time is not the newest one

One number nobody measured pinned a client to the wrong node permanently.

99999993 lets the periodic report move a roamed client, breaking the tie
with conn_time: the later instant of association wins. That is right, and it
has one hole. conn_time 0 means the reporter does not know how long it has
had the client. Read as an instant it becomes time(NULL) - 0 - the newest
association that can exist - so an unmeasured report outbids every measured
one. Worse, once stored as s->assoc_time, every later honest report is
compared against a moment that never happened, and loses.

Measured 2026-09-07, decoded off the wire on the corridor node. Its
Associated Clients TLV:

  BSS 1a:20:7c:74:c7:74  clients=1
    b8:09:8a:50:f2:2a  conn_time = 1185 s

and the controller kept that client filed under itself, because at 07:58:52
its own stale hostapd entry had reported the same client with conn_time 0,
stored as "associated here, just now":

  rep_assoc  = 08:14:45 - 1185 = 07:55:00
  assoc_time =                   07:58:52
  07:55:00 <= 07:58:52  ->  dropped, on every report, for 17 minutes

Nothing would have ended it: the true owner's claim only gets older.

So an unknown conn_time is stored as unknown. A report that cannot say when
the association began does not acquire the strongest possible claim; it takes
ownership only when nothing better is on record, and it leaves assoc_time
unset so a substantiated report can still correct it. This is the same rule
as est_thput and rcpi: a value that was never measured must not read as one.

The drop is also no longer silent. It was the only branch here that said
nothing, and that is why a client sitting on the wrong node for a quarter of
an hour looked like the report never arrived.

Verify: with a client that roamed while its old node still listed it, the
controller's sta row must follow the node that reports a real conn_time, and
`grep "stays on"` must show the comparison when it does not.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -794,7 +794,11 @@
 						s->ssidlen = 0;
 					}
 					s->de_sta->conn_time = conntime;
-					s->assoc_time = time(NULL) - conntime;
+					/* conn_time 0 is "unknown", not "just now" - see
+					 * the move branch below for what recording it as
+					 * an instant costs. */
+					s->assoc_time = conntime ?
+						time(NULL) - conntime : 0;
 
 					memcpy(s->de_sta->bssid, bssid, 6);
 					node_add_sta(n, s);
@@ -858,10 +862,51 @@
 					 * before.
 					 */
 					struct node *old_n;
-					time_t rep_assoc = time(NULL) - conntime;
+					time_t rep_assoc;
 
-					if (s->assoc_time && rep_assoc <= s->assoc_time)
-						continue;
+					/* conn_time 0 means the reporter does not know how
+					 * long the client has been there. Read as an
+					 * instant it becomes time(NULL) - 0, the newest
+					 * association possible, so an unmeasured report
+					 * outbids every measured one - and, once stored,
+					 * makes every later honest report look older than
+					 * the truth for good.
+					 *
+					 * Measured 2026-09-07, decoded off the wire. The
+					 * Mac b8:09:8a:50:f2:2a had been on the corridor
+					 * node for 1185 s and was reported so. The
+					 * controller had it filed under itself because at
+					 * 07:58:52 its own stale hostapd entry reported it
+					 * with conn_time 0, which was stored as "associated
+					 * here, now". From then on:
+					 *
+					 *   rep_assoc  = 08:14:45 - 1185 = 07:55:00
+					 *   assoc_time =                   07:58:52
+					 *   07:55:00 <= 07:58:52  ->  dropped, every time
+					 *
+					 * The client was pinned to the wrong node
+					 * permanently by one number nobody had measured.
+					 */
+					if (!conntime) {
+						if (s->assoc_time)
+							continue;
+						rep_assoc = 0;
+					} else {
+						rep_assoc = time(NULL) - conntime;
+
+						if (s->assoc_time &&
+						    rep_assoc <= s->assoc_time) {
+							cntlr_dbg(LOG_STA, "%s: STA " MACFMT
+								  " stays on " MACFMT ": reported"
+								  " assoc %lld is not later than"
+								  " %lld\n", __func__,
+								  MAC2STR(s->macaddr),
+								  MAC2STR(s->agent_almacaddr),
+								  (long long)rep_assoc,
+								  (long long)s->assoc_time);
+							continue;
+						}
+					}
 
 					cntlr_info(LOG_STA, "%s: STA " MACFMT " is on node "
 						   MACFMT " now (was " MACFMT "), conn_time %u < %u\n",
@@ -879,7 +924,7 @@
 					memcpy(s->de_sta->bssid, bssid, 6);
 					s->state = STA_CONNECTED;
 					s->de_sta->conn_time = conntime;
-					s->assoc_time = time(NULL) - conntime;
+					s->assoc_time = rep_assoc;
 					node_add_sta(n, s);
 				} else {
 					/* Same node. A changed BSSID here is a steer between
@@ -906,8 +951,16 @@
 					 * the branch above compares against it. It is
 					 * time-invariant, so on a settled client this writes
 					 * the same value every time; it moves only when the
-					 * client really re-associated. */
-					s->assoc_time = time(NULL) - conntime;
+					 * client really re-associated.
+					 *
+					 * An unknown conn_time leaves it unknown rather than
+					 * stamping "now": a node that cannot say how long it
+					 * has had the client must not thereby acquire the
+					 * strongest possible claim to it. */
+					if (conntime)
+						s->assoc_time = time(NULL) - conntime;
+					else
+						s->assoc_time = 0;
 				}
 
 				if (bss_iface && bss_iface->bss && bss_iface->bss->is_bbss)
